  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through Thursday)...\n\nWest/Northwest flow prevailed over our region today behind last\nnight shortwave passed through. Satellite imagery showed moisture \nriding this flow into Crazies/Absarokas/Beartooths producing\nmainly orographic snow in the high country. Surface pressure\npatterns and 700mb winds are lined up to produce strong winds \nalong the foothills, mainly focusing on the US-191 corridor \nfrom Big Timber to Judith Gap. Winds have been gusting to XX mph\nin this area. A High Wind Warning continues through 8 PM. \n\nThe next weather system is still progged to move through our area\nlater tonight and Saturday morning. Look for precipitation to pick\nup by evening over our mountains and foothills in south central \nMT and spread generally east/southeast overnight. This shortwave \ntrough is going to track relatively quickly through our region, \nbut cross sections indicate a period of strong frontogenesis \nsetting up over our mountains and foothills south and west of \nBillings late tonight into early Saturday morning along with winds\nturning northerly for a moderately strong upslope. Omega fields \nsuggest good lift in the dendritic layer in the early morning \nSaturday making for efficient snow production. Winds may gust to\naround 30 mph creating blowing snow and reduced visibility. The \nbest forcing stretches from the Crazies region to the Big Horns \nregion and southward. Areas across the plains to the north of this\nline may not see too much precipitation...but could see a mix of \nrain/snow with a dusting over higher hills. The forcing and \nupslope weaken by midday Saturday as the system slides southeast \nwith skies clearing out during the afternoon. \n\nWinter weather highlights continue for the Absaroka/Beartooth \nRange (Winter Storm Warning 8-16 ins), with Winter Weather \nAdvisories going into affect at 8 PM for the foothills around \nLivingston down to Red Lodge (1-6 ins). We also have a Winter \nWeather Advisory starting at 5 pm for the Big Horns (4-8 ins). All\nthese end around midday Saturday. \n\nRidging is expected to move in for Sunday, keeping conditions \ndry. Flow will back from northwest to west/southwest by Sunday \nnight. This will induce lees side pressure falls leading to\nfavorable pattern for gap winds near Livingston/Nye. &00 mb winds\nare weak (<30mph), but the gradient can produce winds approaching\n50 mph. Currently, the chance of gusts over 50 mph for Livingston\nis stands at 30% for late Saturday night into Sunday. Some\nmoisture riding the backing flow may also produce some orographic \nsnow showers over the high country Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. Otherwise, the first half of the week ahead looks rather \nbenign with seasonable temps and just slight chances of \nprecipitation, mainly over the mountains. Ensembles suggest a \nsplit flow trough arriving in the Rockies around Wed/Thu which may\nbring a little better chance of precipitation to the area, but \nnothing to organized. BT",
